<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\StyloMail.Core\StyloMail.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\StyloMail.Queue\StyloMail.Queue.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The protocol reader and the session state machine are internals: they are how the transport
      does its work, not part of what it offers. Their bounds (line length, continuation count,
      byte budget) are correctness properties though, and a bound that is only exercised through a
      whole successful delivery is a bound nobody has ever seen fire. This grants the test assembly
      access so each limit can be driven directly, rather than widening the public surface of a
      library whose API should be "deliver this" and "accept this".
    -->
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleToAttribute">
      <_Parameter1>StyloMail.Transport.Tests</_Parameter1>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately no third-party package references.

    This project writes MIME bytes onto a socket and must preserve them exactly, so the SMTP
    protocol is implemented here rather than delegated to a client library whose serialiser would
    round-trip the message through its own object model. A round-trip can re-fold headers and
    re-encode bodies, which invalidates DKIM at the receiving end; "we did not rewrite the
    message" is a correctness claim we can only make about code we control.

    Owning the state machine is also what makes the delivery outcome taxonomy honest: the
    difference between "we never finished DATA" and "we finished DATA and lost the reply" is a
    property of how far the protocol got, and that is known here and nowhere else.
  -->

</Project>
